    <!-- ACT 1: crisis, physical moment -->
    <h2 id="trap">The Pond Is Also the Trap</h2>

    <p>She limps to the pond at dusk because the burning has become more than she can stand. The water is the only thing that helps. She kneels at the muddy edge, uncovers her ankle, and lets the cool brown water close over the blister.</p>

    <p>Inside her leg, a worm almost three feet long has been growing for a year. The water is exactly what it has been waiting for. The moment the blister touches the pond, the worm pushes its head through the skin and releases a cloud of microscopic larvae. Tomorrow, other women will come with their pots. The cycle starts again.</p>

    <p>That is Guinea worm disease. Its Latin name is <em>Dracunculus medinensis</em>, the little dragon of Medina. There is no vaccine. There is no medicine that kills it once it is inside you. The only treatment on Earth is a small stick and weeks of patience.</p>

    <p>Most people have never heard of it. Here is the strange part. In 1986, it infected an estimated 3.5 million people a year across 21 countries in Africa and Asia. Today it is down to 10 human cases. Not 10 million. Ten. And the world got there without inventing a single new drug.</p>

    <p>You have never worried about a worm coming out of your leg. You have never seen a village where the only water is also the thing that infects you. That is not luck. It is the result of four decades of work by people who decided that a disease of the poorest villages deserved the same effort as a disease of the rich ones.</p>

    <!-- ACT 1: biology -->
    <h2 id="nocure">Three Feet of Worm, and Nothing That Kills It</h2>

    <p>The worm spreads through water fleas the size of a grain of sand. They are called copepods. A pond that has no well, no pipe, and no treatment is full of them. When you drink that water, you swallow the fleas. They die in your stomach. The larvae inside them do not.</p>

    <p>Those larvae cross into your body and grow. Slowly. For about a year they mature in silence. Then the female, now up to three feet long and as thin as a noodle, travels to the surface of your skin, most often on a leg or a foot, and forms a blister. That is when the burning starts.</p>

    <p>Pay attention to this number: the agony can last for weeks. A person with an emerging worm cannot farm, cannot cook, cannot carry water for their children, cannot go to school. If the worm snaps during the traditional attempt to pull it out, the person can go into a violent allergic reaction. Secondary infections are common, and some people lose the use of a limb.</p>

    <p>This is not a new disease. A calcified Guinea worm was found in the abdominal wall of an Egyptian mummy dated to roughly 1000 BC. A Greek writer described it in the second century BC. An anonymous fifteenth-century painting shows Saint Roch, the plague saint, pointing at a worm emerging from his own thigh. Extraction by winding the worm onto a stick has not changed since ancient Greece.</p>

    <p>Here is the key insight, and it is the whole story in one line. The worm needs two things to survive: a human body to grow inside, and open water to escape into. Break either link and the worm dies where it stands.</p>

    <!-- ACT 2 (interleaved): Carter backstory -->
    <h2 id="farmer">The Farm Boy Who Hated a Parasite</h2>

    <p>In the mid-1980s, the Carter Center was a young nonprofit with a vague mission and a famous founder. Jimmy Carter had left the White House four years earlier. The public health experts who had worked in his administration came to him with a plan.</p>

    <p>Their timing was not an accident. In 1980 the World Health Organization had declared smallpox eradicated, the first human disease ever wiped off the planet. If one disease could die, why not a second. Guinea worm, they told him, was the best candidate left on the list.</p>

    <p>Carter said yes, and the decision makes more sense once you know where he came from. He grew up on a farm in Georgia with no electricity and no running water. He had carried water from a well. He knew what it meant when the water was the thing making everyone sick.</p>

    <p>In 1988 he went to Ghana and saw the disease with his own eyes. In one village, nearly 350 people had worms pushing through their skin. He approached a young woman who looked like she was cradling a baby in her arm. Years later he described what he actually found: there was no baby. She was holding her right breast, and a worm was emerging from the nipple.</p>

    <p>He wrote that meeting the victims, especially the children and teenagers, is what made him and his wife Rosalynn step up the campaign rather than wind it down.</p>

    <!-- ACT 2 (interleaved): Hopkins backstory -->
    <h2 id="egypt">The Student Who Went to Egypt for the Antiquities</h2>

    <p>If Carter was the engine, the man who built the machine was a young doctor named Donald Hopkins, and his story starts in a place nobody expects.</p>

    <p>In 1961, Hopkins was an undergraduate at Morehouse College on a trip to Egypt. He went for the pyramids and the tombs. What he noticed instead was eyes. Trachoma, a bacterial infection that scars the eyelid until the lashes scrape the eyeball, was everywhere. He came home with one decision made: he was going to work on tropical diseases for the rest of his life.</p>

    <p>He took the long road into it. A medical degree at the University of Chicago. A master's in public health at Harvard. Then, in 1967, at age 25, he was sent to Sierra Leone to run the smallpox eradication and measles control program, at a moment when the global smallpox campaign was still an experiment nobody was sure would work.</p>

    <p>That job taught him the lesson he would carry for the rest of his career. You do not have to vaccinate everyone on Earth. You have to find every last case and cut the chain around it. Smallpox was declared eradicated in 1980.</p>

    <p>Hopkins kept going. He helped run the campaign in India. He rose to deputy director of the CDC. He wrote a history of smallpox that was nominated for a Pulitzer Prize. And in 1987 he left the CDC for a new job at the Carter Center, to lead the fight against a worm almost nobody outside its villages had heard of.</p>

    <p>He kept a Guinea worm preserved in a jar of formaldehyde on his desk. It was a reminder of the thing he was trying to make the last one of.</p>

    <!-- ACT 2: wrong turns -->
    <h2 id="wrong">The Wrong Turns</h2>

    <p>The plan could not wait for science to rescue it, and that was the first hard lesson. There was no drug, and there was nothing in the pipeline worth betting on. A worm three feet long is not a target a vaccine can easily chase. So the campaign had to be built out of behavior, and behavior is the hardest material in public health.</p>

    <p>However, the bigger setback came from a quiet assumption buried in the plan. In 1991, Hopkins and his technical director Ernesto Ruiz-Tiben published the argument for why this disease was beatable. They listed the reasons it should work: the worm cannot live in a human beyond its one-year incubation, transmission is seasonal, you can see the worm when it comes out, and, as they wrote, "there is no known animal reservoir."</p>

    <p>That last piece was the foundation. The whole strategy rested on the idea that the worm lives only in people. Cut the human chain in every village, and the parasite has nowhere left to go. It was clean. It was logical. And it was wrong.</p>

    <p>In 2012, Guinea worm turned up in dogs in Chad. The dogs were probably eating fish that carried the larvae. Suddenly the worm had a second home, and that home could walk around and drink from any pond it liked.</p>

    <p>Imagine what that does to a plan that is 25 years old and nearly finished. Every dog that carries the worm can put it back into the water a year later. You cannot teach a dog to filter its water. You cannot explain the life cycle to it. The campaign had to start inventing solutions for an animal that no one had thought was part of the problem.</p>

    <p>Then came the setback that hurt most in public. After years of progress, Ghana had a resurgence in 2007. Hospitals filled again. Carter, frustrated and blunt, suggested the disease should be renamed "Ghana worm." It was a hard thing to say about a country, and it landed. Ghana was embarrassed, pushed harder, and recorded its last case in 2010.</p>

    <p>Here is why that matters. The campaign learned that shame, used carefully, could move a government faster than data. That is uncomfortable. It is also true.</p>

    <!-- ACT 2 to 3: the money -->
    <p>Even the cost estimate missed. In 1991 the experts put the price of global eradication at roughly 75 million dollars. Carter ended up raising about 500 million. The worm did not care about the math.</p>

    <!-- ACT 3: the fix -->
    <h2 id="tools">Four Tools, None of Them Medicine</h2>

    <p>Then came the fix, and it is not a cure. It is a system, and every piece of it is boring.</p>

    <p>First, filter every drink. A piece of finely woven cloth stretched over the mouth of a water pot strains out the water fleas carrying the larvae. A plastic pipe filter, a straw you sip through, does the same thing on the road. It costs next to nothing and it shuts the door on infection.</p>

    <p>Second, treat the ponds. A mild larvicide called ABATE is poured into the water sources people drink from. It kills the water fleas, not the people, and it does not last. Teams must return every 28 days, in punishing heat or heavy rain, to clean and re-treat the same pond. In Ethiopia, workers clear the grass and muck out of a pond before every treatment because that is what it takes to protect the drinking water.</p>

    <p>Third, contain every case. The moment someone reports a blister, they move to a case containment center, a compound where they stay until the worm is out. There the worm is wound slowly onto a small stick, a few centimeters a day. If the staff rush and the worm snaps, the patient pays for it. Containing the case means the worm never touches water, and a worm that never touches water has no next host.</p>

    <p>Fourth, pay people to report. A cash reward, often 100 dollars or more, goes to anyone who reports a confirmed case. It sounds crude. It is the reason the program can find 10 cases in a continent. In 2025, national programs investigated more than one million reported cases, almost all of them within 24 hours.</p>

    <p>Underneath all four tools sits the permanent fix: clean water. Boreholes and wells, so the pond stops being the source of life. By December 2024, in Chad, 86 percent of the villages tracked by the program had a source of drinking water free of the fleas.</p>

    <p>This is where it gets interesting. None of these tools kills a single worm inside a single person. They break the chain so completely that the worm runs out of places to hide. Because the worm cannot survive in a human for much more than a year, because it does not spread quietly from person to person, and because it announces itself by crawling out of your skin, a campaign that finds every worm and keeps it away from water can starve the parasite out of existence.</p>

    <p>That is the breakthrough. Not a molecule. A rule: no worm touches water.</p>

    <!-- ACT 3: ceasefire, turning point -->
    <h2 id="ceasefire">The Ceasefire Fought Over a Worm</h2>

    <p>The largest obstacle was not the worm. It was war.</p>

    <p>By 1995, global cases had fallen from 892,055 in 1989 to 129,852. But southern Sudan, in the middle of a civil war, held almost all the rest. Carter wrote in a trip report that Sudan had about three times as many cases as the rest of the world combined. Then he wrote the line that decided the next six months of his life: "Only access to villages in the South can let us deal with the problem."</p>

    <p>He had one thing the generals did not. He was not a soldier. He was a former president with a health program and a reputation for keeping his word. He negotiated with both sides of the war. After the rebel leader John Garang agreed to a truce, Sudan's president Omar al-Bashir relented on March 27, 1995.</p>

    <p>The two sides called it a two-month ceasefire. It held for about six months, and it became the longest humanitarian ceasefire in history. In that window, health workers pushed into villages that had been unreachable for 15 years. They handed out 200,000 cloth filters. They surveyed for the first time the places nobody had been able to count. They also immunized children against measles and other diseases while they were there.</p>

    <p>That is the entire strategy in one image: a filter, a larvicide, a stick, and a truce between enemies to hand out cloth.</p>

    <p>Sudan has been certified free of indigenous Guinea worm since 2002.</p>

    <!-- ACT 4 -->
    <h2 id="numbers">What Ten Cases Mean</h2>

    <p>Here is the delta. In 1986, an estimated 3.5 million people a year across 21 countries. In 1989, when most countries began reporting in earnest, the count was 892,055. By 1995 it was 129,852. By 2003 it was about 32,000, a fall of more than 99 percent in less than two decades.</p>

    <p>Pakistan became the first country to eliminate it, in 1993. Asia was clear of the disease by 1997. In 2009, Nigeria, once the country with the most cases on Earth, reached zero. Hopkins called it a thunderclap. Then the milestones started coming. Chad and the Central African Republic stopped transmission in 1999 and 2001.</p>

    <p>By 2023 the worldwide total of human cases was 14. In 2024 it was 15. And in 2025, the lowest number ever recorded: 10 human cases. Four in Chad, four in Ethiopia, two in South Sudan. Four other countries that had recently reported cases, including Angola, Cameroon, Mali, and the Central African Republic, reported zero humans for the second year in a row.</p>

    <p>The Carter Center estimates the campaign has averted more than 100 million cases since 1986. The World Health Organization has certified 200 countries and territories as free of the disease. Global transmission is still ongoing in six: Angola, Cameroon, Chad, Ethiopia, Mali, and South Sudan.</p>

    <p>Carter raised about 500 million dollars to hunt a worm that would never have touched an American. That may be the most striking number in the story. The disease was never a threat to the people with money. It was a threat to the people without power, and someone with both decided that was enough reason.</p>

    <!-- ACT 4: honest -->
    <h2 id="honest">The Honest Part</h2>

    <p>Here is the honest part. This is not finished, and the ending is harder than the headlines suggest.</p>

    <p>Jimmy Carter died on December 29, 2024, at the age of 100. Fourteen cases were left in the world when he died. He had spent nearly 40 years saying he wanted to outlive the Guinea worm. He did not. Aides said that even in hospice care, he was still asking for the case count, and still getting it.</p>

    <p>The animals remain the biggest problem. About 700 animals were found with Guinea worm in 2025, after the worm was first detected in dogs in Chad in 2012. That number barely moved. Program staff now help build doghouses, tether dogs during the peak season so they cannot contaminate ponds, and push people to bury fish waste instead of leaving it where dogs can find it. It is slow, strange, unglamorous work, and it is the reason zero keeps moving out of reach.</p>

    <p>War is the second problem. You cannot certify a country free of a disease while you cannot safely reach its villages. Mali, South Sudan, and Sudan are among the hardest places on Earth to run a surveillance network, and that is exactly where the last worms live. Peace and health are tangled together here, which is precisely what Carter learned in 1995.</p>

    <p>The third problem is success itself. When cases drop to 10, people stop believing the disease is still there, and they stop reporting. A single missed worm can restart transmission in a village that had three clear years. That is why the reward money matters, and why more than a million reports a year still get investigated.</p>

    <p>Even the finish line has been moved. In 2023, the international commission that certifies eradication redefined what finishing means. It is no longer enough to have zero human cases for three years. The worm must be confirmed gone from both humans and animals. Ten human cases is nearly nothing. It is also not zero.</p>

    <p>Carter said the thing he wanted most in 2015, while he was being treated for cancer, and it has followed the campaign ever since.</p>

    <blockquote>"I would like the last Guinea worm to die before I do."<br>- Jimmy Carter, 2015</blockquote>

    <p>He very nearly got his wish. The worm outlived him by at least a year and counting.</p>

    <!-- ACT 5 -->
    <h2 id="pearl">A Knock on the Door</h2>

    <p>Here is the part that matters. Why should a story about a worm in a pond matter to you, in 2026?</p>

    <p>Because of what it proves about hard problems. Every other disease humanity has wiped out came with a weapon. Smallpox had a vaccine. Rinderpest, the cattle plague, had a vaccine too. The Guinea worm had nothing. No vaccine. No drug. No cure. Not one. The people who drove it from 3.5 million cases to 10 did it with a piece of cloth, a chemical poured into ponds, a small stick, and a cash reward for telling the truth.</p>

    <p>You will never worry about a worm coming out of your leg. You will never see the burning blister, or the pond that is also a trap, or the fields that go unplanted because the farmers cannot walk. That is the point. The fix was not a miracle. It was a decision, repeated a few million times, by people who had almost nothing and decided to keep going anyway.</p>

    <p>Ten worms are left. Somewhere in Ethiopia or Chad or South Sudan, a volunteer is walking to a house for the third time this week with a flipbook of drawings under her arm, checking for a blister that has not appeared yet. That is what the end of a disease looks like up close. Not a cure. Not a headline. A knock on the door.</p>

    <p>If a piece of cloth can erase a 3,000-year-old parasite, then "too big" and "too old" and "too far away" are not reasons. They are excuses. And the people who beat this worm never had time for those.</p>

